// CompilerFreshness — a harness command REFUSES to report on a binary older than the sources it was
// built from.
//
// ⭐ **A VERDICT ABOUT CODE THAT IS NOT IN THE TREE IS WORSE THAN NO VERDICT**, and it is produced by
// one keystroke: `build; suite` (a `;`, not a `&&`) runs the suite whatever the build did, and a
// FAILED BUILD LEAVES THE PREVIOUS BINARY IN PLACE — which is precisely what `E6002`
// (`Compiler.discardPreviousOutput`) exists to say out loud when it can. So the harness has to be able
// to tell for itself, because the caller demonstrably cannot be relied on to.
//
// It goes both ways, and only one of the two directions ever gets noticed:
//
//   * a false RED — measured during rung A1, exactly 4 tests, and that oddly specific number is what
//     identified it as staleness rather than a regression;
//   * a false GREEN — the same lie, in the direction nobody re-checks.
//
// And it is not only the suite: the incident that filed this rung was a whole scaling LADDER read off
// the previous binary after `maxon error-codes check` had failed the build. `scale-test` refuses on
// exactly these terms for that reason.
//
// ============================================================================================
// WHAT IT COMPARES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT
// ============================================================================================
//
// **The sources compiled INTO the binary — `<project>/**.maxon` — and nothing else.**
//
// ⚠ NOT `stdlib/`, although `cross-target-gate.sh`'s `--skip-build` guard does sweep it. That guard
// is about a binary that is ABOUT to be trusted for a build; this one is about a suite, and a suite
// compiles the stdlib modules FROM SOURCE for every fragment it builds. An edited
// `stdlib/*.maxon` is therefore already live in the verdicts — refusing over one would be a FALSE
// REFUSAL, and a false refusal wedges the suite just as thoroughly as a stale binary lies to it.
//
// ⚠ NOT every `.maxon` file under the project root either — only the ones `maxon build` actually
// compiles. `maxon-shv2/track0/` carries a `.maxonignore` and its program is not in the binary, so an
// edit to it cannot change a single verdict. `collectMaxonSources` is what answers that, and is used
// here rather than re-spelled: it excludes the build manifest AND every `.maxonignore`d subtree, which
// is two of the three clauses. The two it does NOT carry — `*.test.maxon` and the compiler's own
// `.maxon/` output directory — are applied on top, HERE, because they are what is left of the gap
// between "a source of this project" and "a source of THIS BINARY".
//
// ⭐ **THE `.maxonignore` CLAUSE USED TO BE APPLIED HERE TOO, AND ITS REMOVAL IS THE POINT.** It was a
// hand-written mirror of `MaxonIgnore.IsIgnored`, in a file whose header already records losing a
// clause off a mirror TWICE. The walk carries it now (measured against the bootstrap on a probe tree —
// see `Compiler.collectMaxonSources`), so a copy here could only ever be a second answer waiting to
// drift from the first. `SourceCollector.ExclusionFor` in the bootstrap remains the authority for what
// went in — the bootstrap is what built this binary — and the walk agreeing with it is now a pinned
// spec (`specs-shv2/maxonignore.md`) rather than an assertion.
//
// ============================================================================================
// TWO THINGS IT STRUCTURALLY CANNOT SEE. NEITHER IS CLAIMED IN THE MESSAGE.
// ============================================================================================
//
// 1. **A CHANGE TO THIS CHECK ITSELF.** The check ships inside the binary it reports on, so a stale
//    binary runs the OLD check. That is fine for the case that matters — edit a source, forget to
//    rebuild, and the old check reads the new mtimes and refuses — and it is exactly why the message
//    says "a source is newer than this binary" rather than anything about being up to date.
// 2. **A DELETED SOURCE.** Nothing gets NEWER when a file goes away, so a build whose input set has
//    SHRUNK reads clean. (The bootstrap's own build cache does record the input set —
//    `<project>/.maxon/cache/build-manifest.json` lists every source path with its mtime — and could
//    answer both questions exactly. Reading it would mean a second parser for a `maxon-sharp`-owned
//    format, which is this project's signature bug wearing a fix's clothes, so it is not done here.)
//
// mtimes are also read at ONE-SECOND resolution — `File.info().modifiedTime` is Unix epoch seconds on
// every target this compiler emits (x64-windows divides a FILETIME by 10,000,000, `EmitFileStat`;
// arm64 reads `st_mtime`'s seconds field, `ARM64CodeEmitter.Runtime`) — so an edit landing in the same
// second the binary was written TIES, and a tie is not "newer". That biases the check toward MISSING a
// staleness rather than inventing one, which is the safe direction here: a false refusal has no
// workaround, and a miss leaves things exactly as they were before this file.
//
// ⚠ The repo's OTHER in-binary staleness check breaks that same tie the OTHER way, on purpose, and the
// two must not be read as an accident: `maxon-dev-mcp/mcp/Staleness.serverFreshness` compares `>=`
// because its refusal costs one rebuild of a small tool nobody else is waiting on, while this one
// wedges an entire suite for a rebuild measured in minutes. Same question, different price on being
// wrong, so a different tie-break — not a rule that drifted.

// `maxon build` writes a project's executable into `<project>/.maxon/`, so a binary whose parent
// directory is NOT that one was not built in place and has no project above it to compare against.
// It is also what a file the compiler WROTE lives under, at any depth, and such a file is not a
// source to anybody — `collectMaxonSources` walks straight into it.
//
// Written in lower case because every comparison against it goes through `caseFolded`. See there.
let CompilerOutputDirName = ".maxon"

// The directory whose presence in the project root says this is a DEVELOPMENT CHECKOUT rather than an
// installed copy. Detected, never guessed from the path's spelling: a released binary's own path can
// be anything at all, and the one fact that distinguishes it is whether its sources are there.
let SourceRootMarkerDirName = "Compiler"

// A `*.test.maxon` holds `test` declarations and is left out of a production build
// (`SourceCollector.ExclusionFor`), so it is not in the binary either.
//
// Written in lower case because the comparison against it goes through `caseFolded`. See there.
let TestFileSuffix = ".test.maxon"

// What one executable's freshness came out as.
//
// TWO arms, not three: "no source tree beside the binary" and "a source tree entirely older than it"
// are genuinely different facts, but every caller does the same thing with both — proceed — and a
// third arm nobody could act on differently would only invite a caller to invent a difference. Which
// of the two it was is visible in `stalenessOf`'s own early returns, where it matters.
union StalenessVerdict
	mayProceed
	stale(newerSource FilePath)
end 'StalenessVerdict'

// Refuse this command if `binary` is older than a source it was built from. Returns the exit code the
// command should return: 0 to go ahead, `NothingRanExitCode` to stop.
//
// `role` names the executable in the message, because two commands ask this about two different
// binaries — `spec-test` about the harness-and-compiler it runs on, `scale-test` about the compiler it
// drives — and "which binary is stale" is the first thing the reader needs.
//
// ⛔ **`spec-test` USED TO ASK IT TWICE, AND THAT PAIR IS GONE (G20).** `refuseIfSpecTestStale` asked
// about the harness AND, when `--compiler=<path>` named a different file, about the compiler under
// test. An in-process compile has exactly one binary to be stale, so there is one question again — and
// the second one had, by its own admission, nothing to ask in an in-tree run.
export function refuseIfStale(binary FilePath, role String) returns ExitCode
	return match stalenessOf(binary) 'verdict'
		mayProceed gives 0
		stale(newerSource) gives reportStale(binary, role: role, newerSource: newerSource)
	end 'verdict'
end 'refuseIfStale'

// Is any source compiled into `binary` newer than it?
//
// The scan stops at the FIRST one, for `cross-target-gate.sh`'s reason: the reader needs an example,
// not an inventory, and the fix is the same however many there are.
function stalenessOf(binary FilePath) returns StalenessVerdict
	let outputDir = try binary.parent() otherwise return StalenessVerdict.mayProceed
	if caseFolded(outputDir.filename()) != CompilerOutputDirName 'notBuiltInPlace'
		return StalenessVerdict.mayProceed
	end 'notBuiltInPlace'

	let sourceRoot = try outputDir.parent() otherwise return StalenessVerdict.mayProceed

	// Bound to a local first: `join` is overloaded on String and FilePath, and a CALL in the argument
	// position does not disambiguate them any more than an inline `try` expression does (E3007).
	let marker = pathComponent(SourceRootMarkerDirName)
	if not Directory.exists(sourceRoot.join(marker)) 'notACheckout'
		return StalenessVerdict.mayProceed
	end 'notACheckout'

	let built = try File.info(binary) otherwise panic("CompilerFreshness: could not read the modification time of {binary.toString()}, a file this command is running from or has already proved exists")

	var sources = MaxonSourcePathArray.create()
	_ = try collectMaxonSources(sourceRoot, into: sources) otherwise panic("CompilerFreshness: could not walk {sourceRoot.toString()} for sources, though its {SourceRootMarkerDirName}/ directory is right there")

	for source in sources 'each'
		let info = try File.info(source) otherwise panic("CompilerFreshness: could not read the modification time of {source.toString()}, a file the directory walk had just listed")

		if info.modifiedTime <= built.modifiedTime 'notNewer'
			continue
		end 'notNewer'

		// Asked ONLY of a file that is already newer, which is the rare case: the common path is one
		// `File.info` per source and no ancestor walk at all.
		if not isCompiledInto(source, sourceRoot: sourceRoot) 'notInTheBinary'
			continue
		end 'notInTheBinary'

		return StalenessVerdict.stale(source)
	end 'each'

	return StalenessVerdict.mayProceed
end 'stalenessOf'

// Whether `maxon build` compiled `source` into the binary, given that `collectMaxonSources` has
// already dropped the build manifest and every `.maxonignore`d subtree. See this file's header for why
// the remaining two clauses live here rather than in that walk.
function isCompiledInto(source FilePath, sourceRoot FilePath) returns bool
	if caseFolded(source.filename()).endsWith(TestFileSuffix) 'testFile'
		return false
	end 'testFile'

	return not isUnderCompilerOutputDirectory(source, sourceRoot: sourceRoot)
end 'isCompiledInto'

// Does `source` sit under the compiler's OWN output directory, at any depth?
//
// A question about a file's ANCESTORS rather than about the file, hence the upward walk. It is the one
// exclusion left that `collectMaxonSources` does not apply itself: that walk recurses into a `.maxon/`
// output directory (PLAN.md A2z), so a `.maxon` file the compiler WROTE still reaches this filter and
// must be stopped here — a file the compiler produced is not a source it was built from.
//
// The walk stops at `sourceRoot`, which is where a file that came out of `collectMaxonSources(sourceRoot)`
// is guaranteed to be under; above it there is nothing this question could be about.
function isUnderCompilerOutputDirectory(source FilePath, sourceRoot FilePath) returns bool
	var dir = try source.parent() otherwise panic("CompilerFreshness: {source.toString()} has no parent directory, though a directory walk found it inside one")

	while true 'upwards'
		if caseFolded(dir.filename()) == CompilerOutputDirName 'compilerOutput'
			return true
		end 'compilerOutput'

		if dir.equals(sourceRoot) 'reachedRoot'
			break
		end 'reachedRoot'

		// A path with no parent left is the filesystem root, which `sourceRoot` is not — so this arm
		// is only reachable if the walk somehow started outside the project, and nothing above it
		// could be an output directory of THIS one.
		dir = try dir.parent() otherwise 'atFilesystemRoot'
			break
		end 'atFilesystemRoot'
	end 'upwards'

	return false
end 'isUnderCompilerOutputDirectory'

// One path component, lower-cased for comparison against `CompilerOutputDirName` or
// `TestFileSuffix` — which are therefore both spelled in lower case.
//
// ⚠ **THE AUTHORITY FOLDS CASE ON EVERY PLATFORM, AND A MIRROR THAT DOES NOT IS A FALSE REFUSAL.**
// `SourceCollector.IsTestFile` and `SourceCollector.IsInCompilerOutputDir` both compare
// `OrdinalIgnoreCase`, so `Api.Test.maxon` is NOT in the binary. MEASURED both ways: the bootstrap
// answers `Skipped 1 test file(s)` for exactly that name, while a byte-exact `endsWith` here called
// it a source of this binary, found it newer and REFUSED THE WHOLE SUITE over a file the build had
// never read. That is the same shape as the `.maxon/` output directory this file's header already
// records — a mirror one clause narrower than the rule it mirrors — and it is the expensive
// direction, because a false refusal has no workaround. (The bootstrap carries its own scar from
// this exact divergence: `SourceCollector.SuggestTestFileName`'s comment records a case-SENSITIVE
// copy of the same suffix leaving `Api.Test.maxon` neither buildable nor testable.)
//
// The `clone()` is load-bearing rather than defensive: `String.toLower` mutates its receiver's buffer
// IN PLACE, and `FilePath.filename()` hands back the FilePath's OWN `path` string for a component
// with no separator in it. Folding without cloning would lower-case somebody else's path — the same
// trap, with the same answer, as `FilePath.canonicalForCompare`.
function caseFolded(component String) returns String
	return component.clone().toLower()
end 'caseFolded'

// A constant path COMPONENT as a FilePath. `join` is overloaded on String and FilePath and a bare
// String constant matches both (E3007), so every component joined in this file is bound to a FilePath
// first — the same disambiguation `LadderIndex.indexedScriptNames` makes.
function pathComponent(name String) returns FilePath
	return try FilePath.from(name) otherwise panic("CompilerFreshness: '{name}' is not a valid path component")
end 'pathComponent'

// Say which binary, which source, and what to do — the three things `cross-target-gate.sh`'s
// equivalent refusal prints, and for its reason: a refusal that says only "STALE" sends the reader off
// to re-derive all three.
//
// It deliberately does NOT claim the binary is otherwise up to date. This check cannot see a change to
// itself and cannot see a DELETED source (see the header), so the only thing it is entitled to report
// is the one file it actually found.
function reportStale(binary FilePath, role String, newerSource FilePath) returns ExitCode
	printError("error: {role} is STALE — a source it was built from is newer than the binary.\n")
	printError("  binary: {binary.toString()}\n")
	printError("  newer:  {newerSource.toString()}\n")
	printError("  Rebuild it (`maxon build maxon-shv2`) and re-run. Nothing was run: every verdict below\n")
	printError("  this line would have been about code that is not in that binary.\n")
	return NothingRanExitCode
end 'reportStale'
